            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Finds the peaks of a grid - cells higher than every other cell in their neighbourhood - and gives their positions and heights, highest first, and how many there are. It is the 2-D (or 3-D) counterpart of scipy.find_peaks.\n\nscipy.label finds regions above a threshold, so two hills that run into each other become one region. local_maxima finds the summits, so two people standing close in an occupancy map, or two bright spots in a camera image, are still two. Use it for hot spots in a heat map, the centres of people in a depth or occupancy grid, the brightest points of an image, the strongest cells of a spectrogram.\n\nIt is built on scipy.ndimage.maximum_filter, which is how peaks in a grid are usually found; SciPy has no function by this name.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.local_maxima <size: int> <threshold: float>\nDefaults: size 3, threshold 0.5.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe grid (any number of axes). Every input is searched and sent.\n\nsize (int box, also an inlet):\nHow wide a neighbourhood a peak must top, in cells: 3 compares each cell with the 8 around it; larger keeps only peaks that stand out over a wider area, and merges close ones.\n\nthreshold (float box, also an inlet):\nPeaks below this are ignored.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\npositions:\nWhere each peak is, row then column (in cells), highest peak first. A flat top gives its middle.\n\nvalues:\nThe height of each.\n\ncount:\nHow many peaks. Sent first, then values, then positions.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nRandom values smoothed into hills (left), new every two seconds. With a neighbourhood of 5 and a threshold of 0.55, the summits are found: the count and their positions are listed, highest first.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.label finds whole regions above a threshold. scipy.find_peaks finds peaks along a line. scipy.gaussian_filter smooths a grid first, so its peaks are hills and not noise.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1150,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Finds the peaks of a grid - cells higher than every other cell in their neighbourhood - and gives their positions and heights, highest first, and how many there are. It is the 2-D (or 3-D) counterpart of scipy.find_peaks.\n\nscipy.label finds regions above a threshold, so two hills that run into each other become one region. local_maxima finds the summits, so two people standing close in an occupancy map, or two bright spots in a camera image, are still two. Use it for hot spots in a heat map, the centres of people in a depth or occupancy grid, the brightest points of an image, the strongest cells of a spectrogram.\n\nIt is built on scipy.ndimage.maximum_filter, which is how peaks in a grid are usually found; SciPy has no function by this name.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.local_maxima <size: int> <threshold: float>\nDefaults: size 3, threshold 0.5.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe grid (any number of axes). Every input is searched and sent.\n\nsize (int box, also an inlet):\nHow wide a neighbourhood a peak must top, in cells: 3 compares each cell with the 8 around it; larger keeps only peaks that stand out over a wider area, and merges close ones.\n\nthreshold (float box, also an inlet):\nPeaks below this are ignored.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\npositions:\nWhere each peak is, row then column (in cells), highest peak first. A flat top gives its middle.\n\nvalues:\nThe height of each.\n\ncount:\nHow many peaks. Sent first, then values, then positions.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nRandom values smoothed into hills (left), new every two seconds. With a neighbourhood of 5 and a threshold of 0.55, the summits are found: the count and their positions are listed, highest first.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.label finds whole regions above a threshold. scipy.find_peaks finds peaks along a line. scipy.gaussian_filter smooths a grid first, so its peaks are hills and not noise."
        },
